    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jarasandha"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jarasandha.jpg" alt="Jarasandha" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jarasandha">Jarasandha</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_painting_from_the_Mahabharata_Balabhadra_fighting_Jarasandha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AnonymousUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> lay south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> in what is now southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, a land of alluvial plains bounded by the river Son to the west and the forested hills of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chota-nagpur-plateau">Chota Nagpur</a> plateau to the south. Its early capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajgir">Girivraja</a> or Rajagriha, the modern Rajgir, stood in a valley enclosed by five hills and was defended by a long wall of rough stone blocks, probably among the oldest surviving fortifications in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atharvaveda">Atharvaveda</a></em> treats the Magadhas as outsiders to Vedic orthodoxy, and the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a></em> preserves legends of an earlier Brihadratha dynasty and its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarasandha">Jarasandha</a>. By the sixth century BCE Magadha was one of the sixteen great realms, or <em>mahajanapadas</em>, listed in Buddhist texts, competing with Kosala, Vatsa, Avanti and the oligarchic republics of the Vajji league. Its rise as a historical power began with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bimbisara">Bimbisara</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryanka-dynasty">Haryanka dynasty</a>, whose reign is traditionally placed c. 544–492 BCE, although many historians, following a later date for the Buddha, place it some decades later. Bimbisara annexed the eastern kingdom of Anga, with its river port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a>, and strengthened his position through marriages with the royal houses of Kosala, which brought him a village of Kashi as dowry, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licchavi">Licchavis</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishali">Vaishali</a> and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madra-kingdom">Madras of the Punjab</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rajgir"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rajgir.jpg" alt="Rajgir" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rajgir">Rajgir</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shanti_Stupa_at_Rajgir_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photo Dharma from Penang, Malaysia, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>'s strength rested on its land. The middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a> received ample rainfall, and the spread of wet-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> cultivation, aided by iron tools, produced surpluses that supported a growing population, towns and a large army. The hills to the south supplied iron ore and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, and their forests sheltered the elephants that became the backbone of Magadhan armies. The period saw what archaeologists call the second urbanisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>: fortified towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajgir">Rajagriha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a> grew along rivers and trade routes, the fine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-black-polished-ware">Northern Black Polished Ware</a> spread as a luxury pottery, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> punch-marked coins came into use. Merchants, some of them organised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a>, traded along the Ganges and the overland routes linking Magadha with Kosala, Kashi and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. Society was divided by <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a></em> and occupation, but Magadha was less bound by Brahmanical orthodoxy than the western Ganges valley, and it became the setting of a remarkable religious ferment. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha">Siddhartha Gautama</a>, the Buddha, attained enlightenment at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya">Bodh Gaya</a> in Magadha and preached at Rajagriha, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bimbisara">Bimbisara</a> gave his order the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veluvana">Veluvana</a> bamboo grove; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Vardhamana Mahavira</a>, the great teacher of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>, also taught in the region, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajivika">Ajivikas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makkhali-gosala">Makkhali Gosala</a> were active there. These movements found many lay supporters among merchants and townspeople.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bimbisara"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bimbisara.jpg" alt="Bimbisara" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bimbisara">Bimbisara</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bimbisara_with_his_royal_cortege_issuing_from_the_city_of_Rajagriha_to_visit_the_Buddha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Biswarup Ganguly, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> built the strongest army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> valley, combining infantry, cavalry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> and large numbers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> drawn from its forests. Its kings fought a long series of wars that steadily absorbed their neighbours. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bimbisara">Bimbisara</a> conquered Anga in the east. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajatashatru">Ajatashatru</a> faced the Vajji league, the confederation of clans around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licchavi">Licchavi</a> city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishali">Vaishali</a> north of the Ganges, and according to Jain tradition fought it for some sixteen years. Jain texts credit him with two new weapons, a catapult that hurled large stones, the <em>mahashilakantaka</em>, and a chariot fitted with swinging maces, the <em>rathamusala</em>, while Buddhist texts tell how his minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassakara">Vassakara</a> sowed discord among the Vajjis before the final campaign. To support the war Ajatashatru fortified the village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataligrama</a> near the meeting of the Ganges and the Son, the future Pataliputra. He also fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prasenajit">Prasenajit</a> of Kosala, Bimbisara's brother-in-law, for control of Kashi, and Kosala itself was absorbed into Magadha within the following generation or so. Fear of an attack by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pradyota">Pradyota</a> of Avanti led Ajatashatru to strengthen the defences of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajgir">Rajagriha</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shishunaga-dynasty">Shishunaga dynasty</a> completed the expansion by conquering Avanti in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, leaving Magadha the dominant power of northern India by the mid-fourth century BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/puranas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/puranas.jpg" alt="Puranas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Page_of_Text,_Folio_from_a_Bhagavata_Purana_(Ancient_Stories_of_the_Lord)_LACMA_M.82.62.1_(1_of_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> was a monarchy in which the king commanded the army, collected taxes and dispensed justice through high officials known in Pali texts as <em>mahamattas</em>, who served as ministers, judges and generals. Buddhist texts describe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bimbisara">Bimbisara</a> summoning the headmen of the 80,000 villages of his realm to an assembly, a conventional figure that nonetheless suggests direct royal contact with rural communities and a reliance on revenue from agriculture. Royal ministers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassakara">Vassakara</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajatashatru">Ajatashatru</a> appear as influential advisers. Unlike the republics of the Vajji league and the Shakyas, governed by assemblies of clan leaders, Magadha concentrated authority in the ruler, which gave it a greater ability to mobilise resources for war. Succession, however, was often violent. Buddhist tradition holds that Ajatashatru imprisoned and starved his father Bimbisara, and the Sri Lankan chronicle <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavamsa">Mahavamsa</a></em> claims that the following kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryanka-dynasty">Haryanka</a> line also killed their fathers, until the people deposed the last of them and raised the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shishunaga-dynasty">Shishunaga</a> to the throne. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shishunaga">Shishunaga</a> founded a new dynasty and probably governed for a time from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishali">Vaishali</a>; his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalashoka">Kalashoka</a>, called Kakavarna in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a>, is said to have returned the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a>. The Puranas and the Buddhist chronicles disagree on the order and reign lengths of these kings, and the chronology of the whole period remains uncertain.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pataliputra.jpg" alt="Pataliputra" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pataliputra6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pyule, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>'s significance lies above all in what it became. Its conquests of Anga, the Vajji league, Kosala and Avanti created the territorial core on which the Nanda Empire and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a> built the first state to rule most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a>, founded on a fortified Magadhan village, remained the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanda-dynasty">Nandas</a> and Mauryas and later an important centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a>. The kingdom's history thus marks the transition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from a world of competing <em>mahajanapadas</em> to imperial unification. It was equally important for religion. The Buddha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a> lived and taught under the protection of the Magadhan kings, and Buddhist tradition places the First Buddhist Council in the Saptaparni cave at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajgir">Rajagriha</a> soon after the Buddha's death, under the patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajatashatru">Ajatashatru</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-buddhist-council">Second Buddhist Council</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishali">Vaishali</a> about a century later, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalashoka">Kalashoka</a>. The language of the region, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadhi-prakrit">Magadhi Prakrit</a>, was later associated with the Buddha's own speech, and Pali, the language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> canon, was long called Magadhi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya">Bodh Gaya</a>, Rajgir and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pavapuri">Pavapuri</a>, where Mahavira died, became places of pilgrimage for Buddhists and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jains</a>, and the very name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> derives from the Buddhist monasteries, or <em>viharas</em>, that later filled the old Magadhan lands.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mahapadma-nanda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahapadma-nanda.jpg" alt="Mahapadma Nanda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mahapadma-nanda">Mahapadma Nanda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:I12_1karshapana_Maghada_1ar_(8482307176).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jean-Michel Moullec from Vern sur Seiche (35, Bretagne), France, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> did not fall to a foreign enemy; it was transformed from within. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalashoka">Kalashoka</a>, who according to a later tradition was stabbed in the throat near his city, power passed to his sons, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shishunaga-dynasty">Shishunaga dynasty</a> seems to have weakened. Around 345 BCE, although the date is uncertain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahapadma-nanda">Mahapadma Nanda</a> overthrew the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shishunaga">Shishunaga</a> king, whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a> name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahanandin">Mahanandin</a>. Indian and Greek sources agree that the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanda-dynasty">Nanda</a> was of humble origin: the Puranas call him the son of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shudra">Shudra</a> woman, and the Roman historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-curtius-rufus">Quintus Curtius Rufus</a> reports that the founder of the dynasty was a barber. The Nanda dynasty ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a> over an empire that stretched across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> valley and probably beyond, and was famed for its wealth and the size of its army, which, according to Greek accounts of the campaign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, helped discourage his soldiers from advancing beyond the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beas">Beas</a> in 326 BCE. About 321 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a>, traditionally guided by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanakya">Chanakya</a>, overthrew the last Nanda king and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>. The kingdom of Magadha thus ended not as a defeated state but as the nucleus of larger empires, and its name lives on in the Magadh region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magahi">Magahi</a>, the language spoken there today.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>, the kingdom of south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haryanka-dynasty">Haryanka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shishunaga-dynasty">Shishunaga</a> dynasties, absorbed its rivals among the great realms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> valley, sheltered the early Buddhist and Jain communities and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a>, creating the core from which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanda-dynasty">Nanda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya</a> empires unified most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>.</em></p>

